{:contract/id :kotobase.disclosure/v1
 :status :implemented-host-adapter
 :architecture-adr "com-junkawasaki/root:90-docs/adr/2609061400-recipient-bound-disclosure.edn"
 :entrypoints [kotobase.disclosure-grant/release!
               kotobase.disclosure-grant/release-async!
               kotobase.disclosure-grant/delivery-verification]
 :identities
 {:resource "Raw CIDv1/SHA-256 of the exact encrypted octets. This is NOT the CID of a decrypted datom or an index block that contains the ciphertext."
  :grant "Canonical kotoba.value.v1 CID of the whole signed grant. Different recipients get different grant CIDs; identical decrypted values remain identical."
  :key-envelope "Canonical value CID of provider metadata plus encoded wrapped-key ciphertext. Referenced by grant; held privately until release."
  :signature "signing-cid(kind, record) domain-separates the unsigned canonical value. Signing and encryption keys are distinct."
  :binding "binding(grant) excludes signature and key-envelope-cid, avoiding a cyclic content address; the crypto provider binds it as authenticated info/AAD."
  :parent "Canonical value CID string of the signed parent. These are application-level references, not IPLD Link values: a generic ipld/links walker does not discover them. The delivery protocol receives a bounded root-to-leaf vector and validates every edge."}
 :issue-order
 ["Encrypt each independently disclosable object with its own random data key. Publish only ciphertext."
  "Authenticate the recipient's signing identity and independently bind its encryption-key fingerprint; public information cannot forge signatures. Human bootstrap remains Passkey-only."
  "Construct grant fields excluding key-envelope-cid/signature, compute binding, and ask the qualified hybrid provider to seal the data key for that recipient using that binding."
  "Include provider metadata, epoch, binding and nonempty encoded octet vector :sealed/ciphertext in the envelope. Compute its value CID and sign the grant with that CID included."
  "Expose the signed grant as appropriate; retain the actual envelope at the delivery service. Publish plaintext or recipient envelopes only if intentionally giving up the corresponding access/audit boundary."
  "Recipient signs the exact grant CID, ciphertext CID, receiving-key fingerprint, tenant, principal, audience, epoch, nonce and expiration."
  "Delivery service supplies trusted context independently, verifies every signature and narrowing edge, admits local policy, atomically consumes the scoped nonce, then signs/commits/rereads before returning the envelope."
  "Recipient validates delivery bindings and trusted executor signature before authenticated unwrapping/decryption. Return delivery receipts to the owner's audit view; CID existence alone is not discovery."]
 :host-ports
 {:context "Exact host-authenticated tenant/owner/principal/recipient-key/resource/policy/audience/executor/now/epoch. Never trust a copy supplied by a requester. Time and epoch describe the decision snapshot."
  :verify! "Resolve key/id + algorithm from trusted principal/tenant/epoch/record-kind state, then verify payload-cid. Unknown, revoked or wrong-role keys fail closed; only literal true succeeds. Supports Promise on async entry."
  :authorize! "Call existing grant/local authority for this exact chain and request, including individual grant revocations and recipient-key status. Receives the whole chain so revoking an ancestor can refuse descendants. Must return literal true."
  :consume-nonce! "Atomic compare-and-insert on tenant/principal/audience/nonce across service replicas. Only literal true is fresh. A spent nonce remains spent after later failures; retries use a newly signed nonce."
  :sign! "Sign the supplied domain-separated payload-cid as the configured executor. Never accept the requester as an executor by default."
  :commit! "Store the signed event durably at its canonical value CID and return {:receipt/durable? true :receipt/cid expected-cid}. No mutable head may select canonical truth."
  :read! "Fetch the committed record by exact receipt CID; must reproduce the signed event. A successful write acknowledgement alone is insufficient."
  :open! "Ayatori host crypto provider: bind the recipient-key fingerprint to the actual local secret key, authenticate binding during unwrap, authenticate content ciphertext, then return Arrangement encoded bytes. Metadata validation does not establish KEM correctness or provider qualification."}
 :envelope-required
 [:envelope/provider :envelope/algorithms :envelope/kem? :envelope/hybrid?
  :envelope/epoch :envelope/binding :sealed/ciphertext]
 :limits
 {:max-chain-length 32
  :resource-scope :one-exact-ciphertext-cid
  :operations #{:decrypt :propose-update}
  :executed-operation :decrypt
  :delegation "Issuer equals parent recipient; child operation set is a subset, time window is contained, depth strictly decreases, tenant/owner/resource/policy/epoch are unchanged."
  :bounds "Ingress must bound wire bytes, collection/string lengths and concurrency before decoding or signature verification. The 32-link limit is not a byte quota."
  :cost "A chain of L links uses L grant verifications plus request and receipt verification on the service; client additionally verifies the receipt. Ciphertext hashing is O(bytes). Each uncached delivery entails durable write/read, so this is not a per-row latency improvement. Batch delivery requires a separate exact-coverage contract."
  :revocation "Reject future official deliveries at current epoch/policy. Already-released envelopes, keys and plaintext remain usable offline."
  :audit "The event is key-envelope-release-authorized: committed before sending, it can exist even if delivery fails. It is not a countersigned receipt, successful-decryption proof, or evidence of human comprehension."
  :privacy "Do not publish raw plaintext CIDs, identities, grant ancestry, or access logs merely to obtain content addressing. Public low-entropy plaintext hashes permit guessing. Protect metadata and define retention."
  :storage "IPFS availability/immutability is not global log completeness. Owner observation, witness/checkpoint distribution, retention and grant discovery remain deployment duties."
  :query "Keep governed-read and visibility checks. Key delivery does not authorise an arbitrary query, expose index/blinding keys, or accept a proposed update."}
 :verification
 {:runtime "nbb 1.5.212 / Node 24.19.0; pinned repository dependencies"
  :kotobase {:tests 13 :assertions 59 :failures 0 :errors 0
             :command "nbb --classpath <kotobase src:test plus pinned dependency src paths> scripts/test-disclosure.cljk"}
  :coverage ["owner-to-recipient and narrowing delegation"
             "wrong parent/delegator, operation/time amplification, wrong recipient and epoch"
             "actual Ed25519 signatures and tampering"
             "Promise sequencing, scoped nonce replay, failed durable write/readback"
             "signed hybrid downgrades and envelope substitution"]
  :not-measured [:jvm-compat-suite :native-kotoba :wasm-build :live-network
                 :hybrid-kem-provider-qualification :production-durable-store]}
 :rollout-gates
 [{:id :key-provider :status :pending
   :required "Connect a qualified existing hybrid provider and actual key custody; verify malformed ciphertext, wrong key, wrong AAD and downgrade failures with provider test vectors."
   :measured
   {:on "2026-09-06"
    :what "The ML-KEM-768 half of the hybrid provider is qualified. kotoba-lang/envelope's envelope.qualify runs the deployed module against known answers generated by a DIFFERENT implementation (BouncyCastle 1.81, via kotoba-lang/security's conformance/crypto/vectors) and hands the evidence to kotoba.security.crypto-policy/evaluate-pq-provider, which returns :pq-provider/qualified? true with no violations. The module digest is pinned over the three @noble/post-quantum files that implement ML-KEM; a mismatch refuses at the point of use. Empty vectors are refused rather than passed, and a tampered known answer fails the check."
    :command "cd orgs/kotoba-lang/envelope && npm run qualify:ml-kem"
    :wire "envelope.sealed-key frames one hybrid wrap as the octet vector this contract carries in :sealed/ciphertext, with binding(grant) as the AEAD AAD. Wrong binding, wrong recipient, substituted encapsulation, malformed frame and a flipped byte in every region are each refused, with tests."
    :consumer "Wired 2026-09-06. ayatori.disclosure-open/recipient-opener builds ayatori's :open! port on that provider: the content key exists once, is wrapped under binding(grant), travels as :sealed/ciphertext, and is recovered through the qualified provider or not at all. A custody store that returns a key for another fingerprint is refused by name rather than as an AEAD failure, and an absent binding is refused rather than used. ayatori suite 131 tests / 399 assertions, zero failures, on nbb 1.5.212 / Node 26.7.0. Each refusal was shown to discriminate by removing it and watching exactly one test go red."
    :not-measured "Key custody: where a recipient's ML-KEM and X25519 secret keys live, and how a host resolves them from :recipient-key. That is now the ayatori opener's :keys! port -- a named seam with nothing behind it, rather than an unstated assumption, but still nothing behind it. @noble/hashes and @noble/curves are outside the digest binding and covered only by the known-answer test. No FIPS validation and no side-channel certification are claimed; evaluate-pq-provider says so itself in :pq-provider/non-claims."}
   :custody "Landed 2026-09-06. envelope.keystore holds a hybrid recipient as a RECORD -- public material, a :key/status and two wraps, one per half, each sealed under an AAD carrying the recipient's fingerprint -- and the fingerprint is DERIVED from the two public keys rather than assigned, so a grant's :recipient-key is something the issuer and the custodian compute the same way from the same bytes. ayatori's :keys! port is envelope.keystore/unlocker over those records. Refusals, each shown to discriminate by removing the check and watching exactly one test go red: unknown fingerprint (:no-key-for-recipient), a status that is not :active (:key-not-active, and the test iterates kotoba.security.key-status's own blocked set rather than a copy), a relabelled or edited record (:fingerprint-mismatch), the two halves exchanged, a wrong unlock secret, and an ML-KEM secret whose embedded encapsulation key disagrees with the record. Absent and revoked reject differently: an audit that cannot tell them apart cannot see a revocation take effect."
   :remaining "Deployment, not implementation. What is unbuilt under this gate is which authenticator supplies the unlock secret -- envelope.passkey describes the WebAuthn PRF path and nothing wires it -- and that is a property of a deployment, so it overlaps the :delivery-host gate rather than adding library work here. Everything this gate names that can be verified in a repository is verified; do not read that as deployed."}
  {:id :delivery-host :status :pending
   :required "Wire trusted principal registry, policy compiler, freshness/nonce ledger and canonical durable store at a real endpoint; verify owner-visible event retrieval."}
  {:id :native-component :status :pending
   :required "Whole-component native/Wasm qualification under fleet Q9 rules. This CLJC adapter is not evidence of native consumer cutover."}
  {:id :advanced-crypto :status :deferred
   :required "PRE only with an explicit offline-owner requirement and per-object scope/collusion analysis. ABE/FE/fingerprinting are separate profiles; no claim that ordinary CID grants trace leaked plaintext."}]}
